WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.379
2:00 AM, a few miles outside the city.

00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:06.450
The car tore down the asphalt at sixty miles
an hour.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:11.160
We kept the beams low, the dark road around
us only illuminated by the occasional street

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.299
lamp overhead.

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:14.299
Things moved unseen in the trees.

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:16.740
An old song rattled off the radio.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.660
The connection was patchy, so it was interrupted
by intermittent spikes of static.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:25.289
It was the kind of night when you knew, deep
down, that anything could happen.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:28.009
You just hoped that “anything” would be
in your favor.

00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:32.220
I’d rolled the window down a few minutes
ago, breathing in all that cold, fresh air

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.190
to stave off the looming specter of sleep.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.489
Thank god I wasn’t the one driving, or things
would’ve gotten deadly way sooner.

00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:43.570
Cops would’ve found us with our bumper wrapped
around a tree, our heads one with the steering

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.850
wheel or the windshield, dead on impact or
from the unforgiving cold overnight.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.920
They might’ve even felt sorry for us, until
they found the case.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.609
Perhaps it would have been better that way.

00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:56.149
At least it would’ve been quick.

00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:00.859
A lot of bad things can happen on lonely state
highways in the dead of night, and we were

00:01:00.859 --> 00:01:05.040
about to find out that just crashing your
car was one of the more mild ones.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.240
Scott was driving.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.000
He was also the one who brokered this whole
deal, I was just coming along to provide backup.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.170
There was a fully-loaded Saturday Night Special
sitting in my inner coat pocket, hoping that

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.280
it wouldn’t see any action tonight, and
a pump-action shotgun sitting in the back

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.110
in case things got really hairy.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:26.100
Deals like this, you either come well prepared
or reckon with the heavy chance you sure as

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.420
hell won’t be leaving.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.590
I never asked Scott how he came into the goods
currently sitting in the briefcase and he

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.350
never offered an explanation, either.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:38.500
He only told me that he’d already secured
a very interested potential buyer from a syndicate

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:39.740
out of town.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.350
Serious people.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.350
Dangerous people.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:47.229
They’d pay top dollar or leave us tied up
in trash bags in a ditch off the side of the

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:48.229
highway.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:51.490
But we both needed money, and we were willing
to take that risk if it meant we could return

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.630
from this deal as a pair of rich men.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.680
The terms of the deal were simple: We’d
drive out onto a certain state highway at

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.009
a little after 2:00 AM with the goods, meet
the buyers at a certain rest stop along the

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:03.500
way, and make the exchange.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.640
We’d then all go our separate ways, and
if we were lucky, none of us would ever see

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.330
each other ever again.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:10.740
Seemed simple enough, sure.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.350
Scott seemed downright chipper about the whole
thing.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.800
And for a little while, I was excited too,
until he told me about the road that the buyers

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.170
wanted to meet us on.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:22.020
We’d all heard legends about that place.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.180
Superstitions, really.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:29.020
People think criminals are scary, but believe
me, we’re a surprisingly superstitious bunch.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.820
Our profession is one largely based on luck.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:36.220
Being in the right place at the right time,
and being lucky enough to avoid the cops along

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.220
the way.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.790
But something you need to know, whether you’re
a criminal or arrow-straight, is that some

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.170
places are always going to be the wrong place.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.470
I’m not gonna tell you which road it is.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.470
I know what you knuckleheads are like.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.470
You’re curious.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.470
You’re thrill-seekers.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.150
Hey, we were all young once.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:57.010
But if I tell you what this road is, I know
you’re gonna try to find it.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.280
Maybe you’ll even decide it’s worth the
trip down for a pleasant Sunday drive.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.650
Ha!

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.200
After what I went through on that road, I
wouldn’t wish a trip down it on my worst

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.200
enemy.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.690
There’s no other word for what we encountered
there other than evil.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.760
When we were kids, we used to call it The
Devil’s Passage.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.220
Every spooky rumor and scary story in the
book circulated about that place.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.990
Lemme see what I can remember…

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.590
Well, there was the Watcher In The Woods.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.510
People used to say that there was a long,
tall figure with the biggest eyes you’d

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:31.420
ever seen - eyes like headlights - standing
amongst the trees.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:36.350
If the moonlight shone in at the right angle
as you were driving past, you’d see it standing

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:41.010
there, just staring at you, thinking about
doing who-knows-what.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.650
Then there was Old Beth, the Ghostly Hitchhiker.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:48.100
People used to say they saw a strange old
woman, hobbling down the side of the road

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.380
in the middle of the night.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.150
Sometimes, people said they could hear her
crying, even if they were far enough away

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:55.510
to make such a thing seem impossible.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:04:00.010
If you took pity on her and pulled over, asking
her if she needed a ride, she’d tell you

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:04.590
that you were a very kind person, but that
she was fine and dandy walking along by herself.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.710
But if you didn’t stop and offer her a lift,
if you just drove away…

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:13.130
Well, local legend had it that the next time
you’d see her face would be in your rearview

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:18.010
mirror, as she sat in your back seat, reaching
for you with her ancient, bony claws.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:22.440
It’d make you think twice about leaving
an old woman to walk home alone in the dark.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.010
And of course, there was the Lone Jogger.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:29.660
The stories my dad told me about him always
used to scare the hell out of me.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.530
He was a pale man, dressed unnaturally light
for the cold winter months, jogging along

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:34.760
the side of the road.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.450
If you looked at him, he’d look back.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:40.390
If your eyes ever met, the stories went that
he’d start running after you.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:45.150
It wouldn’t matter how fast you drove, he’d
somehow always catch up, and stare at you

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.770
through the glass of your car’s windows.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:51.500
He never hurt anyone directly, but I imagine
he probably caused a heart attack or two in

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.880
his time, if he ever really existed.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.940
But all of these were nothing - I repeat,
nothing - compared to the phantom cruiser.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.560
You had to drive cautiously at night, cause
if you didn’t, you might find a ghostly

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.720
1970s police cruiser tailgating you, and that’d
be the worst thing you ever saw.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.070
There were fewer stories about this one than
all the others, because if you ever ran afoul

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:18.090
of the phantom cruiser, chances are that you
wouldn’t survive to tell people about what

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.320
happened to you.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.280
Though people could still make an educated
guess about what happened to you based on

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.830
whatever was left behind.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:29.840
Here’s a not-so-fun fact: The Devil’s
Passage is technically qualified for one of

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.180
the most dangerous highways in the country.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:37.590
From crashes to hitchhiker murders to unexplained
deaths on the side of the road, since 1974,

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:41.550
this road has been an incredibly unpleasant
place to drive.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.330
Every time I saw another horror story about
a strange death on the road, I’d think of

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.330
the Phantom Cruiser.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:50.670
And it was those same thoughts polluting my
brain that night, as Scott drove the two of

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.290
us to the rest stop halfway down the Devil’s
Passage.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:57.720
I only realized I’d dozed off when he nudged
me awake, and the blurry lights of distant

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.389
street lamps flashed into my field of vision.

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:01.840
“Look alive,” he said.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:02.840
“We’re here.”

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.730
The rest stop wasn’t much to look at.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:08.900
All that was there was an abandoned gas station,
really the perfect place for this kind of

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:09.900
illicit deal.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.330
My hand moved instinctively to the Special
in my coat and clicked back the hammer.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.350
Something about this whole setup wasn’t
right.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:17.370
Sting operation?

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:18.390
Police ambush?

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.950
This whole thing reeked of a deal too good
to be true.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:26.190
My instincts turned out to be right, in a
sense, just not in the way I was expecting.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:29.490
As we turned into the rest stop, Scott turned
up his beams.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:34.720
All we saw was carnage waiting for us: A car
- presumably one that once belonged to our

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.180
prospective buyers - in a state of horrific
disarray.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:42.860
It looked as though a train had impacted the
side of the vehicle, completely caving it

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:43.860
in.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.590
The metal was covered in deep scratches and
ruts that looked almost like claw marks.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.270
It had been eviscerated.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:52.420
Scott broke, hard, and we both got out of
the car.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.300
I drew the Special out of my jacket and he
grabbed the shotgun out of the back seat.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.620
We approached with caution, worrying this
might just be another part of the setup until

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.380
we saw the thick puddle of blood congealing
under the driver’s side door.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.610
We drew closer, propelled mostly by morbid
curiosity.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:10.320
Was it a hit from a rival gang looking to
intercept the deal?

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.790
It seemed logical, but there were no bullet
holes in the car.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:17.320
Just ripping, tearing, and massive impact
damage.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:21.430
Scott shined the light of his phone into the
destroyed car, and I vomited when I saw what

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:22.430
was inside.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:27.270
The buyers looked less like people and more
like two stacks of pulled pork in tattered

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.270
clothes.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:31.490
If I hadn’t seen them inside the car, I
wouldn’t have even guessed they were human.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:36.840
And the damage wasn’t just to them: The
upholstery was torn up and burned, with violent

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.560
symbols carved into the walls and scrawled
on the cracked windows in blood.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.440
When I turned to Scott, he was ghost-white,
clutching his phone and the shotgun with trembling

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.440
hands.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:49.850
We didn’t exchange a word, but we both knew
it was time to leave.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:50.930
We could find another buyer.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.120
There would be other opportunities, other
deals.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:54.120
But lives?

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.900
You only get one, and we both silently acknowledged
that if we stuck around here much longer,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:00.770
we wouldn’t even have that.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:05.090
We sped back into the car and locked the doors
behind us - for whatever good that would do,

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.220
considering the damage that’d been done
to the buyers and their car.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:12.050
Perhaps we just needed that illusion of security
to get us the hell out of here.

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:14.770
The car pulled out of the rest stop at break-neck
speed.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.590
Scott floored it, trying to put some good
distance between us and the horror at the

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:19.590
rest stop.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:24.390
Whatever had happened to the Buyers, we didn’t
fancy sharing that same awful fate.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:27.950
My heart dropped down into my guts when I
heard the sirens and the flashing lights behind

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:28.950
us.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.320
After all this, we’d been busted for speeding.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.740
They’d pull us over and find the guns and
the briefcase in the car, and they’d have

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:38.070
a lot of questions that neither of us would
have good answers to.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:41.900
We didn’t know whether to slow down and
hope for the best or speed up and take this

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.390
boy in blue on a genuine car chase.

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.560
This whole thing couldn’t have gone more
wrong.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.529
But my thoughts soon drifted from getting
used to the taste of prison food to something

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:56.320
altogether more sinister: When I saw the car
getting closer in the rearview mirror, I realized

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:59.100
that this wasn’t a modern cop car tailing
us.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:05.700
It was a beat-up old 70s cruiser, traveling
at insane speeds, gaining on us.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.470
The high beams cut through me like razor blades.

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:12.980
I heard the radio crackle into life, even
though neither I nor Scott touched it.

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:18.610
It wasn’t music, just a hoarse, scratchy
voice repeating the word “Run” again and

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:19.610
again.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:23.890
And seeing as whatever was behind us clearly
wasn’t a real cop, we were more than happy

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:25.860
to oblige that request.

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:30.190
Scott hit the gas like our lives depended
on it - Which, to be fair, they did.

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:36.330
But no matter how fast we sped up, the cruiser
kept getting closer, like a demon on our tail.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:40.600
I screamed at Scott to go faster, but we were
going as fast as we could.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.930
Next thing I knew, the phantom cruiser collided
with the back side of our car and sent us

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:48.530
into a spin, showering the two of us with
broken glass crystals as the tires screeched

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:49.530
across the asphalt.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:54.880
It felt like an eternity before the car came
to rest, and at that moment, the phantom cruiser

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:56.280
stopped too.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:57.650
Someone got out.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:02.640
He was dressed like a cop, he even looked
like a cop - a dude in his forties, balding,

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.400
overweight, with a handlebar mustache - but
something was wrong about him.

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:11.870
He didn’t say a word as he approached the
car, and he didn’t seem to register me sliding

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.870
the Special out of my jacket, either.

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:17.870
He was inches away from Scott’s window when
I panicked and opened fire, sending a hail

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:20.050
of small-caliber rounds into his gut.

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:25.830
He stumbled back slightly, as though shocked,
and then everything got a whole lot worse.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.500
The cop let out the most awful bellow.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.710
Not of pain, but of pure rage.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:36.300
Something happened to his face: His eyes started
to glow a bright, hellish red, and his jaw

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:38.920
began to extend until he looked like a Munch
painting.

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:42.520
There were no teeth in there, just an infinite,
black void.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:46.770
He grabbed a dazed Scott through the window,
pulling him into a brutal headlock and dragging

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:51.540
him out of the car, releasing those deranged
bellows the entire time.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:55.410
Scott screamed and pleaded for help; I grabbed
his body and tried to pull him back in, but

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.640
the cop was inhumanly strong.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:01.280
He just kept pulling until he was all the
way out, thrashing on the asphalt.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:02.280
I…

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.610
I don’t want to tell you what he did next.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:06.610
Wouldn’t be right.

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:10.880
But suffice to say, I couldn’t save Scott,
and I definitely didn’t want it happening

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:11.880
to me.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.470
While he was working on Scott, I scrambled
into the driver’s seat and floored it, hoping

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.860
that Scott would at least buy me some time.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:22.410
I was weeping in terror as I drove away into
the dark, leaving my friend to a horrible

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:25.399
fate with the driver of the phantom cruiser.

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:30.390
So you can only imagine how I felt when, a
few minutes later, I heard the sirens again,

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:34.870
and saw the bright lights getting closer behind
me.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:35.970
“Run…Run…Run…”

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:42.580
Now go check out “SCP-002 The Living Room
&amp; the Most Terrifying SCP Rooms” and “The

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:48.509
Secret at the Bottom of SCP-087 - EXPLAINED”
for more SCP videos that’ll give you nightmares!

